                "type": "text",
                "text": "Perfect! Now I have all the information I need. Let me compile a comprehensive answer based on the source code I've reviewed.\n\n## DuxtServer Route Handler API - Complete Analysis\n\nBased on my examination of the Duxt framework source code, here's the exact API used by DuxtServer route handlers:\n\n### 1. **Handler Callback Type**\n\nThe handler receives a **`Request`** object from the Shelf package (from `package:shelf/shelf.dart`).\n\nFor basic handlers registered with `server.get()`, `server.post()`, etc., the type signature is:\n```dart\ntypedef HandlerFunc = FutureOr<Response> Function(Request request);\n```\n\n**Source**: `/Volumes/External/Duxt/duxt/lib/src/server/duxt_server.dart`, lines 502-506\n\n### 2. **Handler Return Type**\n\nThe handler must return a **`Response`** object (from Shelf) or optionally a `FutureOr<Response>` (which can be a Response or Future<Response>).\n\n**Source**: Same file, line 503\n\n### 3. **`json()` Helper Function**\n\nThe `json()` helper is a convenience function defined in duxt_server.dart:\n\n```dart\nResponse json(dynamic data, {int statusCode = 200, Map<String, String>? headers}) {\n  return Response(\n    statusCode,\n    body: jsonEncode(data),\n    headers: {\n      'Content-Type': 'application/json',\n      ...?headers,\n    },\n  );\n}\n```\n\n**Parameters:**\n- `data` (required): Any JSON-serializable object\n- `statusCode` (optional): HTTP status code, defaults to 200\n- `headers` (optional): Additional response headers to merge with Content-Type\n\n**Source**: `/Volumes/External/Duxt/duxt/lib/src/server/duxt_server.dart`, lines 491-500\n\n**Example from posts.dart**:\n```dart\nreturn json({'posts': posts.map((p) => p.toJson()).toList()});\nreturn json({'error': 'Slug required'}, statusCode: 400);\n```\n\n### 4. **Setting Response Headers (Including Set-Cookie)**\n\nThere are two approaches:\n\n**Approach 1 - Direct with Shelf Response:**\n```dart\nResponse(\n  statusCode,\n  body: jsonEncode(data),\n  headers: {\n    'Content-Type': 'application/json',\n    'Set-Cookie': 'session_token=$token; HttpOnly; SameSite=Strict',\n  },\n);\n```\n\n**Approach 2 - Using ApiResponse.withHeader() extension** (used in auth.dart):\n```dart\nreturn ApiResponse.created(\n  {'user': user.toSafeMap()},\n).withHeader('Set-Cookie', 'session_token=$token; $_cookieFlags');\n```\n\n**Source**: `/Users/flakerimi/TMP/todo/server/api/auth.dart`, lines 49-51, 75-77\n\n**Note**: The `withHeader` method appears to be a helper extension (possibly added to the generated code or via a custom extension). The underlying mechanism uses the `setHeader` helper function defined in api_handler.dart (lines 217-225) which creates a new ApiResponse with merged headers.\n\n### 5. **Accessing Parsed JSON Body from Request**\n\nThere are three ways depending on the handler type:\n\n**For Shelf Request handlers (with `json()` helper):**\n```dart\nserver.post('/api/todos', (req) async {\n  final body = req.body as Map<String, dynamic>?;\n  // body is null if jsonBody() middleware wasn't used\n});\n```\n\nThe `RequestExtensions` on Shelf's Request provides:\n```dart\nextension RequestExtensions on Request {\n  dynamic get body => context['body'];  // Requires jsonBody() middleware\n  String? get rawBody => context['rawBody'] as String?;\n}\n```\n\n**Source**: `/Volumes/External/Duxt/duxt/lib/src/server/duxt_server.dart`, lines 473-488\n\n**For ApiResponse handlers (ApiRequest):**\n```dart\nserver.post('/api/auth/login', (req) async {\n  final body = req.json;  // Direct property access\n  final email = body['email'] as String?;\n});\n```\n\nThe `ApiRequest` class provides:\n```dart\nclass ApiRequest {\n  final String? body;  // Raw string body\n  \n  dynamic get json {\n    if (body == null || body!.isEmpty) return null;\n    return jsonDecode(body!);\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**Source**: `/Volumes/External/Duxt/duxt/lib/src/server/api_handler.dart`, lines 51-82\n\n### 6. **`req.context` - Passing Data Between Middleware and Handlers**\n\nYes, the Shelf `Request` object has a `context` property (Map<String, dynamic>) that middleware can populate:\n\n**In middleware:**\n```dart\nreturn innerHandler(request.change(context: {\n  ...request.context,\n  'body': json,\n  'rawBody': body,\n}));\n```\n\n**In handlers:**\n```dart\nfinal user = req.context['user'] as User;\nfinal params = req.context['params'] as Map<String, String>;\n```\n\n**Source**: `/Volumes/External/Duxt/duxt/lib/src/server/duxt_server.dart`, lines 129-133, 389-393\n\n**Real example from auth middleware**:\n```dart\n// In middleware:\nreq.context['user'] = user;\n\n// In handler:\nfinal user = req.context['user'] as User;\n```\n\n**Source**: `/Users/flakerimi/TMP/todo/server/middleware/auth_middleware.dart`, lines 7-22\n\n### 7. **`req.headers` - Header Access**\n\nHeaders are accessed as a Map<String, String>:\n\n```dart\n// Raw Shelf Request\nfinal cookieHeader = req.headers['cookie'] ?? '';\nfinal accept = request.headers['accept'] ?? '';\n\n// ApiRequest (from api_handler.dart)\nfinal token = request.headers['session_token'];\nString? getHeader(String key) => headers[key.toLowerCase()];  // Case-insensitive lookup\n```\n\n**Source**: \n- Shelf Request: `/Volumes/External/Duxt/duxt/lib/src/server/duxt_server.dart`, lines 161, 253\n- ApiRequest: `/Volumes/External/Duxt/duxt/lib/src/server/api_handler.dart`, lines 56, 81\n\n### Summary of Two Handler Patterns\n\n**Pattern 1: Direct Shelf (simpler, used in posts.dart)**\n```dart\nserver.get('/api/posts', (req) async {\n  final body = req.body as Map<String, dynamic>?;  // Requires jsonBody() middleware\n  return json({'posts': ...});\n});\n```\n\n**Pattern 2: ApiHandler wrapper (more structured, used in todos.dart)**\n```dart\nvoid registerTodoApi(DuxtServer server) {\n  server.post('/api/todos', (req) async {\n    // Note: This uses ApiRequest internally via _handleApi\n  });\n}\n```\n\nBoth ultimately work with Shelf's `Request` and `Response` types at the transport layer."
