  "response_text": "Based on the search results, here are the key JWT authentication best practices for Node.js in 2025-2026:\n\n## Key Best Practices\n\n**Secret Key Management**\nNever hardcode your secret key in your code or store it in a publicly accessible location. Store your secure secret key securely, such as using environment variables or a reliable secrets manager.\n\n**Token Expiration**\nAccess tokens should have a short expiration time, typically between 15 minutes to 1 hour, because access tokens are used to access protected routes and should be short-lived to minimize damage in case of token theft. Refresh tokens should have a longer expiration time, typically between 1 day to 1 week.\n\n**Refresh Token Security**\nRefresh tokens are highly recommended for managing long-lived sessions securely, and should be stored in HTTP-only, secure cookies to minimize exposure to client-side JavaScript. Use Refresh Token Rotation: Every time a refresh token is used, issue a new one and invalidate the old one.\n\n**Token Storage**\nHTTP-Only Cookies cannot be accessed by JavaScript, are protected from XSS, can use Secure + SameSite policies, and are ideal for browser-based apps—this is the most secure storage method for web apps.\n\n**Token Revocation**\nImplement a revoke mechanism: Maintain a server-side blacklist or a database to track active/invalidated tokens. Add a unique identifier (jti) to each JWT and track them in a server-side database to detect and prevent reuse of revoked tokens.\n\n**Additional Security Measures**\nRegularly update JWT libraries to ensure you're protected against known vulnerabilities. In a production environment, you should use HTTPS to secure communication between the client and server.",
