  "response_text": "Based on the web search results, here's a comprehensive overview of JWT signing algorithms and their best practices:\n\n## Algorithm Comparison\n\n**HS256 (HMAC with SHA-256)**\nHS256 is a symmetric keyed hashing algorithm that uses one secret key for both generating the signature and validating it. HS256 is faster than both RS256 and ES256, making it ideal for applications that require rapid processing of JWTs, and the quick computation time translates to lower latency, which is crucial in performance-sensitive applications.\n\n**RS256 (RSA with SHA-256)**\nRS256 is an asymmetric algorithm that uses a public/private key pair, where the identity provider has a private key to generate the signature and the receiver of the JWT uses a public key to validate the JWT signature. RS256 is the recommended algorithm when signing your JWTs as it is more secure, and you can rotate keys quickly if they are compromised.\n\n**ES256 (ECDSA with SHA-256)**\nES256 is another asymmetric algorithm but based on elliptic curve cryptography. ES256 is faster than RS256 due to the efficiency of elliptic curve operations and provides strong security with a reduced computational load compared to RSA algorithms. A short Elliptic Curve (EC) key of around 256 bits provides the same security as a 3072 bit RSA key.\n\n## Best Practice Recommendations\n\nThe best algorithm depends on your architecture and trust boundaries; there is no single \"best\" choice for every application, but use HS256 for simple, single-service setups and RS256 when multiple services need to verify tokens.\n\nIf you're working with microservices, distributed systems, or public APIs where JWTs need to be securely shared and verified without exposing secrets, RS256 is a safe and popular choice and is widely used in scenarios like OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect.\n\nIf performance is a concern, or you're building for mobile apps or constrained devices, ES256 offers a good balance of security and efficiency and is becoming more popular in modern applications every day, especially when working with mobile devices or environments where performance and security are both crucial.",
  "response_type": "stream",
  "response_status": 200
},
