            "tool_use_id": "toolu_01DcQUQvu7y16bNTsRn9tBPa",
            "type": "tool_result",
            "content": "Web search results for query: \"JWT token storage httpOnly cookies vs localStorage security 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"JWT Storage in React: Local Storage vs Cookies Security Battle\",\"url\":\"https://cybersierra.co/blog/react-jwt-storage-guide/\"},{\"title\":\"How to Store JWT Token Securely in LocalStorage vs Cookies\",\"url\":\"https://www.c-sharpcorner.com/article/how-to-store-jwt-token-securely-in-localstorage-vs-cookies/\"},{\"title\":\"Local Storage vs Cookies: Securely Store Session Tokens\",\"url\":\"https://www.pivotpointsecurity.com/local-storage-versus-cookies-which-to-use-to-securely-store-session-tokens/\"},{\"title\":\"LocalStorage vs Cookies: the best-practice guide to storing JWT tokens securely in your front-end - Cyber Chief\",\"url\":\"https://www.cyberchief.ai/2023/05/secure-jwt-token-storage.html\"},{\"title\":\"Choosing Between Local Storage and HttpOnly Cookies for Storing JWT Tokens | by Dennis Cha | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/@cjun1775/choosing-between-local-storage-and-httponly-cookies-for-storing-jwt-tokens-47f4ecbca6ee\"},{\"title\":\"LocalStorage vs Cookies: All You Need To Know About Storing JWT Tokens Securely in The Front-End - DEV Community\",\"url\":\"https://dev.to/cotter/localstorage-vs-cookies-all-you-need-to-know-about-storing-jwt-tokens-securely-in-the-front-end-15id\"},{\"title\":\"Understanding Token Storage: Local Storage vs HttpOnly Cookies - Wisp CMS\",\"url\":\"https://www.wisp.blog/blog/understanding-token-storage-local-storage-vs-httponly-cookies\"},{\"title\":\"Comparing JWT Authentication Strategies: HTTP-Only Cookies vs LocalStorage | by Nijat Aliyev | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://medium.com/@developer.nijat/comparing-jwt-authentication-strategies-http-only-cookies-vs-localstorage-05254ed99722\"},{\"title\":\"Cookies vs. Local Storage: What’s the Difference? When and Where to Use Each?\",\"url\":\"https://www.permit.io/blog/cookies-vs-local-storage\"},{\"title\":\"The Best Place for Your JWTs - Comparing Local Storage and Cookies\",\"url\":\"https://www.linkedin.com/pulse/best-place-your-jwts-comparing-local-storage-cookies-atkinson-lerue\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's a comprehensive overview of JWT token storage security in 2025:\n\n## Key Security Comparison\n\n### HttpOnly Cookies - Security Advantages\n\nHttpOnly cookies offer better protection against XSS but need CSRF defense. If you're using httpOnly and secure cookies, your cookies cannot be accessed using JavaScript, which means even if an attacker can run JS on your site, they can't read your access token from the cookie.\n\nThe OWASP community recommends storing tokens using cookies because of its many secure configuration options.\n\n### LocalStorage - Main Vulnerability\n\nlocalStorage is considered insecure for storing JWTs primarily because it is highly vulnerable to Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks. If an attacker can inject malicious JavaScript into your application, they can easily read the contents of localStorage and steal the JWT, giving them the ability to impersonate the user and access their protected data.\n\n## 2025 Best Practice Recommendations\n\nFor maximum security in 2025 and beyond, use HttpOnly cookies with CSRF protection.\n\nThe most sophisticated approach is a hybrid strategy: Store the short-lived access token in memory (e.g., React state or context) and the long-lived refresh token in a secure, HttpOnly cookie, which provides the best defense against both XSS and CSRF attacks.\n\n### Key Security Features of HttpOnly Cookies\n\nThe HttpOnly flag prevents JavaScript from accessing the cookie, reducing exposure to cross-site scripting (XSS) attacks, while SameSite restricts cookies from being sent with cross-site requests, mitigating cross-site request forgery (CSRF) attacks.\n\n## Trade-offs\n\nWhile HttpOnly cookies offer superior security, they have limitations: Cookies have a size limit of 4KB, so if you're using a big JWT Token, storing in the cookie is not an option.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."
