  "response_text": "Based on the search results, here's what I found about JWT EdDSA Ed25519 signing algorithms and modern recommendations for 2025:\n\n## Modern Recommendation\n\nEdDSA is recommended over ECDSA because it's faster, more secure, and easier to implement. The algorithm preference ranking is: EdDSA > ECDSA or RSASSA-PSS > RSASSA-PKCS1-v1_5.\n\n## Key Advantages of EdDSA/Ed25519\n\nEd25519 uses a 255-bit curve (32-byte private key, 32-byte public key, 64-byte signature) and signing uses SHA-512. Ed25519 provides 128-bit security.\n\nSeveral security benefits stand out:\n\n- **Deterministic signatures**: EdDSA always generates the same signature for the same message, reducing the risk of vulnerabilities related to weak random number generation, which has been a known issue with ECDSA.\n\n- **Performance**: Benchmarks consistently show that EdDSA (especially Ed25519) outperforms ECDSA in both signing and verification speeds, making it ideal for modern applications.\n\n- **Compact tokens**: EdDSA signatures have a length of 512-bits for Ed25519.\n\n## 2025 Standards Recognition\n\nAccording to ENISA's 2023 Algorithms, Key Sizes and Parameters Report, EdDSA is recommended for new systems, while ECDSA remains essential for backward compatibility. EdDSA is listed as one of the approved algorithms in the draft specification of FAPI 2.0.\n\nThe consensus is clear: **use EdDSA/Ed25519 for new JWT implementations** unless you have specific compatibility requirements with legacy systems.",
  "response_type": "stream",
