            "content": "Web search results for query: \"jsonwebtoken vs jose npm Node.js JWT library comparison 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"jws vs jwa vs jose vs jsonwebtoken vs node-jose | JavaScript JWT and JWK Libraries\",\"url\":\"https://npm-compare.com/jose,jsonwebtoken,jwa,jws,node-jose\"},{\"title\":\"jwa vs jose vs jsonwebtoken vs passport-jwt vs express-jwt | JWT Handling Libraries for Node.js Authentication\",\"url\":\"https://npm-compare.com/express-jwt,jose,jsonwebtoken,jwa,passport-jwt\"},{\"title\":\"Jose vs Jsonwebtoken — Why you should switch? | by Joodi | Medium\",\"url\":\"https://joodi.medium.com/jose-vs-jsonwebtoken-why-you-should-switch-4f50dfa3554c\"},{\"title\":\"JWT.IO - JSON Web Tokens Libraries\",\"url\":\"https://www.jwt.io/libraries\"},{\"title\":\"Why You Should Delete jsonwebtoken in 2025 ⭐ - DEV Community\",\"url\":\"https://dev.to/silentwatcher_95/why-you-should-delete-jsonwebtoken-in-2025-1o7n\"},{\"title\":\"jose - npm\",\"url\":\"https://www.npmjs.com/package/jose\"},{\"title\":\"GitHub - panva/jose: JWA, JWS, JWE, JWT, JWK, JWKS for Node.js, Browser, Cloudflare Workers, Deno, Bun, and other Web-interoperable runtimes\",\"url\":\"https://github.com/panva/jose\"},{\"title\":\"Top Node.js Libraries for Implementing JSON Web Tokens (JWT)\",\"url\":\"https://moldstud.com/articles/p-top-nodejs-libraries-for-implementing-json-web-tokens-jwt\"},{\"title\":\"How To Use JSON Web Tokens (JWTs) in Express.js | DigitalOcean\",\"url\":\"https://www.digitalocean.com/community/tutorials/nodejs-jwt-expressjs\"},{\"title\":\"jose vs jsonwebtoken | JSON Web Token (JWT) Libraries\",\"url\":\"https://npm-compare.com/jose,jsonwebtoken\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's a comprehensive comparison of **jose** vs **jsonwebtoken** npm JWT libraries for Node.js in 2025:\n\n## Key Differences\n\n### Maintenance & Modern Support\njose is actively maintained, supports modern JavaScript (ESM, TypeScript), and aligns with web platform standards, making it the best choice for new projects requiring strong standards compliance. In contrast, jsonwebtoken remains stable and widely used, but development has slowed.\n\n### Standards Compliance\nJOSE is comprehensive and supports a wide range of JOSE standards (JWT, JWS, JWE, JWK) and requires advanced features like key management and encryption, making it well-suited for applications that need robust security features and compliance with various standards. Meanwhile, jsonwebtoken is a straightforward solution to create and verify JWTs and is lightweight and easy to use, making it ideal for projects that require basic JWT functionalities without the overhead of additional features.\n\n### Encryption Support\nA major differentiator is encryption: The jose library fully supports both JWT signing (JWS) and encryption (JWE), making it a more versatile choice for applications that require secure token transmission. However, jsonwebtoken does not natively support JWT encryption (JWE); it focuses primarily on signing and verifying tokens (JWS), and for encryption, developers would need to use additional libraries or implement custom solutions.\n\n### Code Patterns\nThe jsonwebtoken library still uses legacy patterns like callbacks and CommonJS modules, which don't align with modern JavaScript projects. In contrast, jose is built with TypeScript from the ground up, providing intelligent autocompletion, type safety, and better developer experience.\n\n### Cross-Platform Support\nJose is designed to work across various Web-interoperable runtimes including Node.js, browsers, Cloudflare Workers, Deno, Bun, and others.\n\n## Recommendations\n\nFor new greenfield projects: Start with jose—it's future-proof, secure by default, and works everywhere. However, for existing Express apps using simple HMAC tokens: jsonwebtoken + express-jwt is battle-tested and sufficient.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."
