            "content": "Web search results for query: \"JWT EdDSA Ed25519 signing algorithm modern recommendation 2025\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"JWTs: Which Signing Algorithm Should I Use?\",\"url\":\"https://www.scottbrady.io/jose/jwts-which-signing-algorithm-should-i-use\"},{\"title\":\"HMAC vs. RSA vs. ECDSA: Which algorithm should you use to sign JWTs? — WorkOS\",\"url\":\"https://workos.com/blog/hmac-vs-rsa-vs-ecdsa-which-algorithm-should-you-use-to-sign-jwts\"},{\"title\":\"JWT with EdDSA / Ed25519 signature\",\"url\":\"https://connect2id.com/products/nimbus-jose-jwt/examples/jwt-with-eddsa\"},{\"title\":\"Easy Guide to JWT Signatures Using EdDSA | Curity\",\"url\":\"https://curity.io/resources/learn/jwt-signatures/\"},{\"title\":\"Digital Signature Algorithms — PyJWT 2.11.0 documentation\",\"url\":\"https://pyjwt.readthedocs.io/en/stable/algorithms.html\"},{\"title\":\"Digital Signatures 2025: ECDSA vs EdDSA\",\"url\":\"https://www.onlinehashcrack.com/guides/cryptography-algorithms/digital-signatures-2025-ecdsa-vs-eddsa.php\"},{\"title\":\"RSA Key Sizes, ECDSA, Curve Choices: What’s Safe In 2025 - Newsoftwares.net Blog\",\"url\":\"https://www.newsoftwares.net/blog/rsa-key-sizes-ecdsa-curve-choices-whats-safe-in-2025/\"},{\"title\":\"JWS with EdDSA / Ed25519\",\"url\":\"https://connect2id.com/products/nimbus-jose-jwt/examples/jws-with-eddsa\"},{\"title\":\"EdDSA (Edwards-curve Digital Signature Algorithm): Definition & Security Context | PentesterLab Glossary\",\"url\":\"https://pentesterlab.com/glossary/eddsa\"},{\"title\":\"Comparing SSH Keys: RSA, DSA, ECDSA, or EdDSA?\",\"url\":\"https://goteleport.com/blog/comparing-ssh-keys/\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, here's what the modern recommendations are for JWT signing with EdDSA and Ed25519 in 2025:\n\n## Modern Recommendation\n\nEdDSA is recommended over ECDSA because it's faster, more secure, and easier to implement. The algorithm ranking recommendation is: EdDSA > ECDSA or RSASSA-PSS > RSASSA-PKCS1-v1_5\n\n## Ed25519 Specifications\n\nEd25519 is a 255-bit curve using Curve25519, with a 32-byte private key, 32-byte public key, and 64-byte signature. Signing uses SHA-512 and Ed25519 provides 128-bit security.\n\n## Key Advantages\n\nJWTs signed with EdDSA have a deterministic signature, meaning that the same JWT header & payload will always generate the same signature. This addresses security concerns with ECDSA that relies on random nonce values. Benchmarks consistently show that EdDSA (especially Ed25519) outperforms ECDSA in both signing and verification speeds, making it ideal for modern applications.\n\nEdDSA signatures have a length of 512-bits for Ed25519, while RSA signatures are at least 2048-bits (about 2-4 times bigger), resulting in significantly smaller EdDSA tokens.\n\n## Standards Recognition\n\nEdDSA is listed as one of the approved algorithms in the draft specification of FAPI 2.0. EdDSA is recommended for new systems, while ECDSA remains essential for backward compatibility.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."
