                "type": "text",
                "text": "Here is a comprehensive summary of JWT authentication best practices for Node.js in 2025/2026, based on current industry guidance.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Libraries and Packages\n\n### `jose` -- The Modern Standard\n\nThe `jose` library (by panva) is the clear frontrunner for new projects. It supports the full JOSE specification suite (JWT, JWS, JWE, JWK, JWKS), is written in TypeScript from the ground up, has zero dependencies, exports tree-shakeable ESM, and runs on Node.js, Deno, Bun, Cloudflare Workers, and browsers. It natively supports both signing (JWS) and encryption (JWE), which `jsonwebtoken` does not.\n\n**Recommendation:** Use `jose` for all new projects.\n\n### `jsonwebtoken` -- The Legacy Workhorse\n\nStill the most downloaded JWT package on npm, but development has effectively stalled. It uses legacy patterns (callbacks, CommonJS), lacks TypeScript types natively, and does not support JWE (encryption). It remains functional and battle-tested for existing Express apps using simple HMAC tokens, but is not recommended for greenfield work.\n\n### `passport-jwt` / `express-jwt`\n\nThese are middleware wrappers. `passport-jwt` integrates JWT verification into the Passport.js strategy ecosystem; `express-jwt` is a lightweight Express middleware for token validation. Both are useful integration layers but are not JWT implementations themselves -- they delegate to `jsonwebtoken` or `jose` under the hood.\n\n### Higher-Level Frameworks\n\n- **Better Auth** -- A comprehensive, framework-agnostic TypeScript authentication framework. It handles the full auth lifecycle (email/password, OAuth, 2FA, passkeys, multi-tenancy, SSO) with built-in database adapters and auto-migration. Auth.js (formerly NextAuth.js) is now maintained under the Better Auth umbrella. This is the recommended approach if you want to avoid building auth plumbing from scratch.\n- **Arctic + Oslo** -- Lightweight, runtime-agnostic OAuth 2.0 client library (50+ providers) built on Oslo's crypto/auth utilities. Good for OAuth-focused flows rather than full JWT management.\n- **Lucia** -- Deprecated as of March 2025; now exists only as a learning resource.\n\n| Library | Pros | Cons |\n|---|---|---|\n| `jose` | Modern, typed, cross-runtime, full JOSE suite, zero deps, active maintenance | Smaller community than jsonwebtoken (growing fast) |\n| `jsonwebtoken` | Massive adoption, simple API, battle-tested | Stale development, no ESM/TS, no JWE, callback-based |\n| `passport-jwt` | Integrates with Passport ecosystem | Extra abstraction layer, Passport itself is aging |\n| Better Auth | Full-stack auth solution, framework-agnostic, plugin ecosystem | Heavier dependency, less control over JWT internals |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Access Token + Refresh Token Pattern\n\nThe standard dual-token architecture:\n\n- **Access token**: Short-lived (5--15 minutes). Sent with every API request. Contains user claims. Stateless verification on the server.\n- **Refresh token**: Longer-lived (7--14 days). Used only to obtain new access tokens. Stored server-side (hashed) and in an httpOnly cookie on the client.\n\n**Flow:**\n\n1. User authenticates -> server issues both tokens\n2. Client sends access token with each request (in `Authorization` header or cookie)\n3. When access token expires, client hits `/refresh` endpoint with refresh token\n4. Server validates refresh token, issues new access + refresh token pair\n5. Old refresh token is invalidated (rotation)\n\n**Key detail:** Store a SHA-256 hash of the refresh token in the database, never the raw token itself. This way a database breach does not directly expose usable tokens.\n\n---\n\n## 3. Token Storage\n\n### httpOnly Cookies (Recommended for web apps)\n\n| Aspect | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| XSS protection | JavaScript cannot read httpOnly cookies |\n| CSRF risk | Must add CSRF protection (SameSite=Strict/Lax, CSRF tokens) |\n| Auto-sent | Browser sends cookies automatically with requests |\n| Size limit | 4KB per cookie |\n\nSet these flags: `httpOnly: true`, `secure: true` (production), `sameSite: 'strict'` or `'lax'`, and scope the path narrowly (e.g., `/api/auth/refresh` for refresh tokens).\n\n### localStorage\n\n| Aspect | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| XSS risk | Fully accessible to JavaScript -- any XSS vulnerability leaks the token |\n| CSRF risk | Not auto-sent, so no CSRF concern |\n| Capacity | ~5-10MB |\n\nOWASP explicitly advises against storing sensitive tokens in localStorage due to XSS exposure. Avoid for production applications.\n\n### In-Memory (Hybrid approach -- most secure for SPAs)\n\nThe emerging best practice for single-page applications:\n\n- **Access token** stored in JavaScript memory (React state/context, a closure variable). Lost on page refresh -- that is by design.\n- **Refresh token** stored in a secure httpOnly cookie scoped to the refresh endpoint.\n- On page load/refresh, a silent call to the refresh endpoint re-establishes the access token.\n\nThis provides the strongest defense against both XSS (access token not in any persistent storage) and CSRF (refresh cookie is narrowly scoped and only grants new tokens, not API access).\n\n---\n\n## 4. Signing Algorithms\n\n### Algorithm Comparison\n\n| Algorithm | Type | Key Size | Signature Size | Speed | Use Case |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **HS256** | Symmetric (HMAC) | Shared secret | 256-bit | Fastest | Single-service, internal only |\n| **RS256** | Asymmetric (RSA) | 2048+ bit keypair | 2048+ bit | Slowest | Distributed systems, OAuth/OIDC |\n| **ES256** | Asymmetric (ECDSA) | P-256 curve | 512-bit | Fast | Mobile, size-constrained, modern APIs |\n| **EdDSA (Ed25519)** | Asymmetric (EdDSA) | Curve25519 | 512-bit | Fastest asymmetric | New systems, cutting-edge security |\n\n### Recommendations\n\n- **For new systems in 2025/2026:** EdDSA with Ed25519 is the top recommendation. It is deterministic (no random nonce vulnerability like ECDSA), produces compact signatures (64 bytes), is faster than both RSA and ECDSA, provides 128-bit security, and is recognized in the FAPI 2.0 draft specification. The `jose` library fully supports it.\n\n- **For distributed systems / OAuth / OIDC compatibility:** RS256 remains the safe, widely-compatible default. Most identity providers (Auth0, Okta, etc.) use RS256.\n\n- **For simple internal services:** HS256 is fine when you control both signing and verification and can securely share the secret. But question whether you even need JWTs in this scenario -- server-side sessions may be simpler.\n\n- **Avoid:** RS384/RS512 (marginal security gain, much larger signatures), and never allow the `none` algorithm. Always validate the `alg` header server-side.\n\n---\n\n## 5. Token Rotation and Revocation\n\n### Refresh Token Rotation\n\nEvery time a refresh token is used, issue a new refresh token and invalidate the old one. This limits the damage window if a refresh token is stolen.\n\n**Reuse detection** is critical: assign each refresh token family an ID. If the server receives a refresh token that has already been used (replay), immediately invalidate the entire token family and force re-authentication. This catches scenarios where an attacker and legitimate user are both trying to use cloned tokens.\n\n### Revocation Strategies\n\n| Strategy | Pros | Cons |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Short-lived access tokens only** | Truly stateless, no revocation infrastructure needed | Up to N minutes of continued access after compromise (where N = token lifetime) |\n| **Redis blacklist/deny list** | Fast lookups, real-time revocation | Breaks pure statelessness, requires Redis infrastructure |\n| **Database-backed blacklist** | Persistent, auditable | Slower than Redis, adds DB load to every request |\n| **Token versioning (per-user counter)** | Revoke all tokens for a user by incrementing their version | Requires a DB lookup per request |\n\n**Practical recommendation:** Use short-lived access tokens (5--15 minutes) combined with refresh token rotation. For immediate revocation needs (e.g., user clicks \"log out of all devices\"), maintain a lightweight Redis-based deny list keyed by the token's `jti` (JWT ID) claim, with entries that auto-expire when the token would have expired naturally.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Modern/Emerging Approaches\n\n### `jose` as the de facto standard\nThe shift from `jsonwebtoken` to `jose` is well underway. `jose` aligns with Web Crypto API standards and works identically across all JavaScript runtimes.\n\n### Better Auth / Auth.js consolidation\nThe Auth.js project (formerly NextAuth.js) has merged under Better Auth, creating a unified, comprehensive authentication framework for the TypeScript ecosystem. This is the direction for developers who want auth as a solved problem rather than building JWT plumbing manually.\n\n### EdDSA adoption\nEd25519-based JWT signing is gaining traction as the most modern and performant asymmetric option, though RS256 remains dominant in existing OAuth/OIDC infrastructure.\n\n### Backend-for-Frontend (BFF) pattern\nFor SPAs, the trend is moving toward a thin backend proxy that handles token exchange, keeping tokens entirely on the server side. The SPA communicates with the BFF using traditional session cookies, and the BFF attaches the JWT when proxying to APIs. This eliminates client-side token storage entirely.\n\n### Passkeys and WebAuthn\nWhile not JWT-specific, passkey-based authentication (FIDO2/WebAuthn) is increasingly seen as the long-term replacement for password-based flows. Better Auth and Auth.js both support passkeys natively. JWTs remain relevant as the session/API token format after initial authentication.\n\n---\n\n## Summary of Practical Recommendations\n\n1. **Library:** Use `jose` for JWT operations. Consider Better Auth if you want a complete auth framework.\n2. **Token pattern:** Short-lived access tokens (5--15 min) + refresh tokens (7--14 days) with rotation and reuse detection.\n3. **Storage:** httpOnly cookies for refresh tokens; in-memory for access tokens in SPAs. Never localStorage for sensitive tokens.\n4. **Algorithm:** EdDSA (Ed25519) for new systems, RS256 for OAuth/OIDC compatibility, HS256 only for simple internal services.\n5. **Revocation:** Rely on short token lifetimes as the primary mechanism; add Redis-based deny lists for immediate revocation needs.\n6. **Architecture:** Consider the BFF pattern for SPAs to keep tokens entirely server-side.\n\n---\n\nSources:\n- [Why You Should Delete jsonwebtoken in 2025 - DEV Community](https://dev.to/silentwatcher_95/why-you-should-delete-jsonwebtoken-in-2025-1o7n)\n- [Jose vs Jsonwebtoken -- Why you should switch? - Medium](https://joodi.medium.com/jose-vs-jsonwebtoken-why-you-should-switch-4f50dfa3554c)\n- [jose - npm](https://www.npmjs.com/package/jose)\n- [GitHub - panva/jose](https://github.com/panva/jose)\n- [JWTs: Which Signing Algorithm Should I Use? - Scott Brady](https://www.scottbrady.io/jose/jwts-which-signing-algorithm-should-i-use)\n- [HMAC vs RSA vs ECDSA -- WorkOS](https://workos.com/blog/hmac-vs-rsa-vs-ecdsa-which-algorithm-should-you-use-to-sign-jwts)\n- [JWT Algorithm Types Explained - Flipper File](https://flipperfile.com/developer-guides/security/jwt-algorithm-types/)\n- [RS256 vs HS256 -- SuperTokens](https://supertokens.com/blog/rs256-vs-hs256)\n- [JWT Storage: LocalStorage vs Cookies - Cyber Chief](https://www.cyberchief.ai/2023/05/secure-jwt-token-storage.html)\n- [LocalStorage vs Cookies for JWT - DEV Community](https://dev.to/cotter/localstorage-vs-cookies-all-you-need-to-know-about-storing-jwt-tokens-securely-in-the-front-end-15id)\n- [Revoking Access with a JWT Blacklist - SuperTokens](https://supertokens.com/blog/revoking-access-with-a-jwt-blacklist)\n- [Using Redis for Token Blacklisting in Node.js - DEV Community](https://dev.to/mr_cea/using-redis-for-token-blacklisting-in-node-js-42g7)\n- [How to Handle JWT Revocation - OneUptime](https://oneuptime.com/blog/post/2026-02-02-jwt-revocation/view)\n- [Secure Authentication with JWTs & Rotating Refresh Tokens - DEV Community](https://dev.to/wiljeder/secure-authentication-with-jwts-rotating-refresh-tokens-typescript-express-vanilla-js-4f41)\n- [JWT Authentication Security Guide: Refresh Token Rotation](https://jsschools.com/web_dev/jwt-authentication-security-guide-refresh-token/)\n- [Refresh Token Rotation Best Practices - Serverion](https://www.serverion.com/uncategorized/refresh-token-rotation-best-practices-for-developers/)\n- [Better Auth](https://www.better-auth.com/)\n- [Auth.js is now part of Better Auth](https://www.better-auth.com/blog/authjs-joins-better-auth)\n- [Securing Node.js Applications in 2025 - Medium](https://habtesoft.medium.com/securing-your-node-js-applications-in-2025-best-practices-for-authentication-and-authorization-0bd574ae3bb3)\n- [5 JWT Authentication Best Practices for Node.js - Medium](https://medium.com/deno-the-complete-reference/5-jwt-authentication-best-practices-for-node-js-apps-f1aaceda3f81)\n- [JWT Signatures Using EdDSA - Curity](https://curity.io/resources/learn/jwt-signatures/)\n- [JWT Security Best Practices - Curity](https://curity.io/resources/learn/jwt-best-practices/)\n- [Digital Signatures 2025: ECDSA vs EdDSA](https://www.onlinehashcrack.com/guides/cryptography-algorithms/digital-signatures-2025-ecdsa-vs-eddsa.php)"
