            "text": "## JWT Authentication in Node.js — Summary\n\n### Libraries\n\n| Library | Status | Recommendation |\n|---------|--------|----------------|\n| **`jose`** | Modern, typed, zero deps, cross-runtime, full JOSE suite | **Use for new projects** |\n| `jsonwebtoken` | Most downloads but stale, no ESM/TS, no JWE | Legacy only |\n| `passport-jwt` / `express-jwt` | Middleware wrappers (delegate to above) | Integration layer |\n| **Better Auth** | Full-stack auth framework (absorbed Auth.js/NextAuth) | Best if you want auth \"solved\" |\n\n### Token Pattern\n\n- **Access token**: short-lived (5–15 min), sent with every request, stateless verification\n- **Refresh token**: longer-lived (7–14 days), stored hashed in DB, used only to get new access tokens\n- **Rotation**: every refresh issues a new pair; detect reuse to catch stolen tokens\n\n### Token Storage (Client-Side)\n\n| Approach | XSS Safe? | CSRF Safe? | Verdict |\n|----------|-----------|------------|---------|\n| **httpOnly cookie** | Yes | Needs SameSite/CSRF token | **Best for web apps** |\n| localStorage | No | Yes | **Avoid** (OWASP advises against) |\n| **In-memory + httpOnly refresh cookie** | Best | Best | **Best for SPAs** |\n\n### Signing Algorithms\n\n| Algorithm | Type | Best For |\n|-----------|------|----------|\n| **EdDSA (Ed25519)** | Asymmetric | **New systems** — fastest asymmetric, compact, no nonce vulnerability |\n| RS256 | Asymmetric (RSA) | OAuth/OIDC compatibility |\n| ES256 | Asymmetric (ECDSA) | Mobile/size-constrained |\n| HS256 | Symmetric | Simple internal services only |\n\n### Revocation Strategies\n\n1. **Short token lifetimes** — primary mechanism (5–15 min access tokens)\n2. **Redis deny list** — for immediate revocation (e.g., \"log out all devices\")\n3. **Token versioning** — per-user counter, increment to revoke all tokens\n\n### Modern Trends\n\n- **`jose`** replacing `jsonwebtoken` as the standard\n- **BFF (Backend-for-Frontend)** pattern keeps tokens entirely server-side for SPAs\n- **Passkeys/WebAuthn** emerging as the long-term replacement for password flows\n- **EdDSA** gaining traction over RS256 for new systems\n\n---\n\nBack to your Duxt project — the server code is compiling clean. Want me to run `duxt dev` and test the endpoints?\n\nSources:\n- [jose - npm](https://www.npmjs.com/package/jose)\n- [Better Auth](https://www.better-auth.com/)\n- [Express | endoflife.date](https://endoflife.date/express)\n- [Why You Should Delete jsonwebtoken in 2025](https://dev.to/silentwatcher_95/why-you-should-delete-jsonwebtoken-in-2025-1o7n)\n- [JWTs: Which Signing Algorithm Should I Use?](https://www.scottbrady.io/jose/jwts-which-signing-algorithm-should-i-use)\n- [JWT Best Practices - Curity](https://curity.io/resources/learn/jwt-best-practices/)"
