            "text": "## 1. Security Review\n\nFound **25 issues** across 5 severity levels:\n\n### Critical (2)\n\n| # | File | Issue |\n|---|------|-------|\n| 1 | `server/api/posts.dart` | **Posts API is completely unauthenticated** — scaffolded CRUD has no auth checks. Anyone can create/update/delete posts. |\n| 2 | `server/api/auth.dart:7` | **Missing `Secure` flag on session cookie** — tokens sent over plain HTTP, interceptable via MITM. |\n\n### High (6)\n\n| # | File | Issue |\n|---|------|-------|\n| 3 | `auth.dart:33` | **User enumeration** — \"Email already registered\" reveals account existence |\n| 4 | `auth.dart` | **No rate limiting** on login/register — enables brute-force attacks |\n| 5 | `auth.dart:21` | **No email format validation** — any string accepted as email |\n| 6 | Multiple | **No input length limits** — strings can exceed DB column limits, causing leaky errors |\n| 7 | `auth.dart:24` | **Weak password policy** — 6 chars minimum, no complexity requirements |\n| 8 | `auth.dart` + `password.dart` | **No max password length** — potential bcrypt DoS (bcrypt truncates at 72 bytes) |\n\n### Medium (9)\n\n| # | File | Issue |\n|---|------|-------|\n| 9 | `auth_middleware.dart:19` | **Fragile cookie parser** — splits on `=`, breaks if token ever contains `=` |\n| 10 | `session_manager.dart` | **No background session cleanup** — expired sessions accumulate forever |\n| 11 | `session_manager.dart` | **No \"logout all devices\"** — no bulk session invalidation |\n| 12 | `session_manager.dart` | **Unlimited sessions per user** — no cap on concurrent sessions |\n| 13 | `main.dart:18` | **Hardcoded CORS origin** — `localhost:4000` only, should be env-configurable |\n| 14 | `posts.dart:33` | **Error details in stdout** — `print('Create failed: $e')` leaks internals |\n| 15 | `user.dart:25-29` | **`toMap()` includes password hash** — accidental serialization risk |\n| 16 | `db.dart:17` | **Unsanitized DATA_DIR** — path traversal via env variable |\n| 17 | Multiple | **No CSRF tokens** — `SameSite=Strict` alone isn't complete CSRF protection |\n\n### Low (5)\n\n| # | File | Issue |\n|---|------|-------|\n| 18 | `auth.dart:75` | **Timing attack on login** — no dummy bcrypt when user doesn't exist |\n| 19 | `todo.dart:31-37` | **`user_id` exposed** in API responses — leaks internal IDs |\n| 20 | `pubspec.yaml` | **Unpinned dependencies** — most have no version constraints |\n| 21 | Models | **No `created_at` default** — nullable with no fallback |\n| 22 | `session.dart` + `session_manager.dart` | **Session tokens stored in plaintext** — should store SHA-256 hash |\n\n### Info (3)\n- No security event logging (failed logins, etc.)\n- No `Content-Type` validation on incoming requests\n- No graceful shutdown handler (risk of SQLite corruption)\n\n**Priority:** Fix #1 and #2 immediately. Then address rate limiting (#4), input validation (#5-8), and cookie parsing (#9).\n\n---\n\n## 2. Multi-Tenant SaaS Database Schema with RBAC\n\nHere's a step-by-step design:\n\n### Step 1: Tenants (Organizations)\n\nEvery SaaS needs isolated tenants. Each tenant is an organization/company.\n\n```\ntenants\n├── id              UUID, PK\n├── name            VARCHAR(255), NOT NULL\n├── slug            VARCHAR(100), UNIQUE, NOT NULL  -- subdomain/URL identifier\n├── plan            VARCHAR(50), DEFAULT 'free'     -- billing tier\n├── settings        JSONB, DEFAULT '{}'             -- tenant-specific config\n├── is_active       BOOLEAN, DEFAULT true\n├── created_at      TIMESTAMPTZ\n└── updated_at      TIMESTAMPTZ\n```\n\n**Reasoning:** UUID primary keys prevent enumeration. `slug` enables subdomain-based routing (`acme.app.com`). `settings` JSONB avoids schema changes for per-tenant config.\n\n### Step 2: Users (Global Identity)\n\nUsers exist globally but belong to tenants through memberships.\n\n```\nusers\n├── id              UUID, PK\n├── email           VARCHAR(255), UNIQUE, NOT NULL\n├── password_hash   VARCHAR(255), NOT NULL\n├── name            VARCHAR(100), NOT NULL\n├── is_active       BOOLEAN, DEFAULT true\n├── email_verified  BOOLEAN, DEFAULT false\n├── last_login_at   TIMESTAMPTZ\n├── created_at      TIMESTAMPTZ\n└── updated_at      TIMESTAMPTZ\n```\n\n**Reasoning:** Users are decoupled from tenants — one person can belong to multiple orgs (like Slack/GitHub). Email is globally unique for login.\n\n### Step 3: Roles (Per-Tenant Role Definitions)\n\n```\nroles\n├── id              UUID, PK\n├── tenant_id       UUID, FK → tenants.id, NULLABLE  -- NULL = system-wide role\n├── name            VARCHAR(50), NOT NULL\n├── description     TEXT\n├── is_system       BOOLEAN, DEFAULT false  -- built-in vs custom\n├── created_at      TIMESTAMPTZ\n└── UNIQUE(tenant_id, name)\n```\n\n**Reasoning:** `tenant_id = NULL` means a global/system role (e.g., `super_admin`). Tenants can create custom roles. `is_system` prevents deletion of built-in roles like `owner`, `admin`, `member`.\n\n### Step 4: Permissions (Granular Actions)\n\n```\npermissions\n├── id              UUID, PK\n├── resource        VARCHAR(100), NOT NULL  -- e.g., 'projects', 'billing', 'users'\n├── action          VARCHAR(50), NOT NULL   -- e.g., 'create', 'read', 'update', 'delete'\n├── description     TEXT\n└── UNIQUE(resource, action)\n```\n\n**Reasoning:** Separating resource + action enables fine-grained checks like `can(user, 'projects', 'delete')`. This is more flexible than hardcoded permission strings.\n\n### Step 5: Role-Permission Mapping\n\n```\nrole_permissions\n├── role_id         UUID, FK → roles.id, ON DELETE CASCADE\n├── permission_id   UUID, FK → permissions.id, ON DELETE CASCADE\n└── PRIMARY KEY(role_id, permission_id)\n```\n\n**Reasoning:** Many-to-many join table. An `admin` role might have `[projects:*, users:read, users:invite]` while `member` has only `[projects:read, projects:update]`.\n\n### Step 6: Tenant Memberships (User ↔ Tenant ↔ Role)\n\n```\ntenant_memberships\n├── id              UUID, PK\n├── tenant_id       UUID, FK → tenants.id, ON DELETE CASCADE\n├── user_id         UUID, FK → users.id, ON DELETE CASCADE\n├── role_id         UUID, FK → roles.id\n├── invited_by      UUID, FK → users.id, NULLABLE\n├── joined_at       TIMESTAMPTZ\n├── created_at      TIMESTAMPTZ\n└── UNIQUE(tenant_id, user_id)  -- one role per tenant per user\n```\n\n**Reasoning:** This is the core junction table. A user can be `admin` in Org A and `member` in Org B. The `UNIQUE(tenant_id, user_id)` constraint enforces one role per org (if you need multiple roles per user per tenant, remove this and make it a many-to-many with a `membership_roles` join table instead).\n\n### Step 7: Row-Level Tenant Isolation\n\nEvery tenant-scoped data table gets a `tenant_id`:\n\n```\nprojects                        -- example business data\n├── id              UUID, PK\n├── tenant_id       UUID, FK → tenants.id, NOT NULL  -- isolation key\n├── name            VARCHAR(255)\n├── created_by      UUID, FK → users.id\n├── created_at      TIMESTAMPTZ\n└── INDEX(tenant_id)            -- every query filters on this\n```\n\n**Reasoning:** Row-level isolation (shared database, shared schema) is the most cost-effective multi-tenant strategy. Every query MUST include `WHERE tenant_id = ?`. Add a PostgreSQL Row-Level Security (RLS) policy as a safety net:\n\n```sql\nALTER TABLE projects ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;\nCREATE POLICY tenant_isolation ON projects\n  USING (tenant_id = current_setting('app.current_tenant')::uuid);\n```\n\n### Step 8: Audit Log\n\n```\naudit_logs\n├── id              UUID, PK\n├── tenant_id       UUID, FK → tenants.id\n├── user_id         UUID, FK → users.id\n├── action          VARCHAR(50)     -- 'create', 'update', 'delete', 'login'\n├── resource_type   VARCHAR(100)    -- 'project', 'user', 'role'\n├── resource_id     UUID\n├── changes         JSONB           -- {before: {...}, after: {...}}\n├── ip_address      INET\n├── created_at      TIMESTAMPTZ\n└── INDEX(tenant_id, created_at)\n```\n\n**Reasoning:** Required for compliance (SOC 2, GDPR). JSONB `changes` captures what changed without schema coupling.\n\n### Entity Relationship Summary\n\n```\ntenants ──┬── tenant_memberships ──┬── users\n          │                        └── roles ── role_permissions ── permissions\n          ├── projects (tenant-scoped data)\n          └── audit_logs\n```\n\n### Authorization Check Flow\n\n```\n1. User authenticates → get user_id\n2. Determine current tenant (from subdomain, header, or session)\n3. Look up tenant_membership WHERE user_id AND tenant_id → get role_id\n4. Look up role_permissions WHERE role_id → get permission_ids\n5. Check if requested (resource, action) exists in user's permissions\n6. Cache the permission set per session (invalidate on role change)\n```\n\n### Key Design Decisions\n\n| Decision | Choice | Why |\n|----------|--------|-----|\n| Isolation strategy | Shared DB, shared schema + RLS | Cost-effective, easy to manage, scales to thousands of tenants |\n| User-Tenant relationship | Many-to-many via memberships | Users can belong to multiple orgs |\n| Role model | Per-tenant custom roles + system roles | Tenants can customize without affecting others |\n| Permission granularity | Resource + Action pairs | Flexible, composable, easy to check programmatically |\n| IDs | UUID v4 | Prevents enumeration, safe in URLs, works across distributed systems |\n\n---\n\nWant me to fix any of the security issues found in the review?",
